{"text":"Function-based Labels (2025) provides a practical rubric separating same-function substitutes, directional replenishment, necessary combinations, usefulness-enhancing complements, unrelated pairs, and ambiguous relationships. Three annotators assessed each pair; detailed labels improved LLM–human alignment, reaching 0.849 macro-F1 after consolidation into three classes. This supports auditing functional relationships rather than treating co-purchases or text similarity as proof of complementarity. Its evidence comes from stationery and household goods; transfer to electronics and entertainment requires validation. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2507.03945v1)

For this task, a proposed adaptation is to annotate separate dimensions: recent-purpose match, exact duplicate versus functional substitute, complementary function, and compatibility supported/contradicted/unknown. Require evidence spans from available titles, descriptions, histories, or actual feedback. Compatibility needs explicit model, connector, format, or specification evidence; missing evidence remains unknown. Historical interactions establish observed behavior, not ownership, satisfaction, current intent, or personality. Report purpose matches as tentative unless feedback explicitly establishes the purpose. Keep unknown rates and evidence coverage visible alongside mismatch rates; these are proposed audit rules, not a validated published instrument.

Validate proxies on a category-stratified sample with independent human labels, clear examples, and adjudication. Measure inter-annotator agreement, per-label precision/recall, macro-F1, and unknown coverage; inspect duplicate/complement and compatibility confusions separately. ConSiDERS (2024) emphasizes explicit scoring criteria and cognitive-bias controls. RecSys 2026 research finds moderate LLM–human ranking correlation but low absolute rating agreement, so fluent explanations or consistent automated judgments cannot substitute for human calibration and factual checks. [ConSiDERS](https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.63/), [LLM evaluation study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01627)"}
